module

public import Mathlib.Algebra.MvPolynomial.Basic
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.MvPolynomial.Homogeneous
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Localization.AtPrime.Basic
public import WeilConjectures.RingTheory.GradedAlgebra.Standard

/-! # Additive generators of a projective order algebra

Source: Hironaka, Additive groups associated with points of a projective space,
Theorem 2; Dietel (2014), Definition 9.1.3 and Lemma 9.1.4, pp.107–108.
(blueprint: lem-trf-projective-order-algebra-additive-generators).

The carrier characterization below writes the finite direct sum of the order
spaces using the homogeneous components of a polynomial. The two tuples in
the additivity identity are indexed by the two summands of `Fin N ⊕ Fin N`.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

namespace Hironaka

/-- The algebra of forms whose local order is at least their degree is graded
and generated by its positive-degree homogeneous additive polynomials.

Source: Hironaka, Additive groups associated with points of a projective space,
Theorem 2; Dietel (2014), Definition 9.1.3 and Lemma 9.1.4, pp.107–108.
(blueprint: lem-trf-projective-order-algebra-additive-generators).

Proof (the eight steps of the accepted DAG split):
1. Put A=P_q and m=qA, using def-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime. Each U_d
is the inverse image of m^d under the linear map P_d to A, hence a vector
subspace. The zeroth power is the unit ideal, so U_0=k. Products of forms
in U_d and U_e have degree d+e and image in m^(d+e). Thus their finite
homogeneous sums form a graded k-subalgebra U.
2. Define D_B f as the coefficient of W^B in f(Z+W). On a monomial Z^E
this is binom(E,B) Z^(E-B) when B is componentwise at most E, and zero
otherwise. Expanding products gives the Hasse Leibniz formula. The commutator
with multiplication by g is the sum of D_C(g) D_(B-C) for nonzero C at
most B. Induction on |B| bounds the differential order by |B|. Sums and
multiplication of the output preserve this bound by the same commutator
induction. The node lem-trf-differential-operator-localization therefore
extends D_B to A with the same order.
3. An operator E of order at most r on a commutative algebra maps I^d into
I^max(d-r,0). For r=0 it is multiplication by E(1). For r>0 and d<=r the
claim is automatic. For d>r write a generator of I^d as ab with a in I
and b in I^(d-1). In E(ab)=aE(b)+[E,a](b), induction on d handles the
first term and induction on r handles the second; both lie in I^(d-r).
Additivity handles sums. This proves the claim by induction on r and d.
4. Apply this to m and the localized D_B. For f in U_d and |B|<=d the
image of D_B f lies in m^(d-|B|), and its degree is d-|B|. For |B|>d
it is zero. Thus U is stable under every Hasse derivative, including those
whose order is divisible by the characteristic; no factorial is inverted.
5. Let K=k[T], contained in U. Homogeneity of the generators grades K by
weighted degrees of expressions in them. If K differs from U, take the
least d of disagreement. Constants are in K as it is a k-algebra, and
every degree-one form is additive, so d>=2. Order degree-d monomials
lexicographically. Row reduction gives a basis of K_d with distinct leading
exponents. Subtracting these rows from an element of U_d outside K_d gives
f outside K_d with zero coefficient at every leading exponent of K_d.
There are finitely many degree-d monomials, so elimination terminates.
6. This f cannot be additive. Take the largest nonadditive exponent E in
its support. Mixed terms from different original exponents cannot cancel,
since their two exponents sum to the original exponent. Hence E=B+C for
nonzero B,C of degrees strictly between zero and d, with binom(E,B) and
binom(E,C) equal and nonzero in k. Every additive degree-d monomial is
killed by both D_B and D_C, by comparing the mixed coefficients.
7. By steps 2–4, D_B f and D_C f belong to U in degrees below d, hence
to K by minimality. Their leading exponents are C and B: larger terms of
f are additive and killed, and subtraction of a fixed multiindex preserves
lexicographic order among surviving terms. Their leading coefficients are
nonzero. Their product g belongs to K_d, since its degree is
(d-|B|)+(d-|C|)=d, and has leading exponent E. All other products have
smaller exponent and the leading coefficient is a product of nonzero field
elements. The normalization in step 5 forces the coefficient of f at E to
vanish, a contradiction.
8. There is therefore no degree of disagreement. Equality degree by degree
and finite homogeneous decompositions give U=K=k[T], as claimed. -/
theorem projective_order_algebra_additive_generators
    {k : Type u} [Field k] (N : ℕ) (hN : 1 ≤ N)
    (q : Ideal (MvPolynomial (Fin N) k)) [q.IsPrime]
    (hq : @Ideal.IsHomogeneous _ _ _ _ _ _
      (MvPolynomial.homogeneousSubmodule (Fin N) k) _ _ MvPolynomial.gradedAlgebra q)
    (hrel : ¬ @HomogeneousIdeal.toIdeal _ _ _ _ _ _
      (MvPolynomial.homogeneousSubmodule (Fin N) k) _ _ MvPolynomial.gradedAlgebra
      (@HomogeneousIdeal.irrelevant _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _
        (MvPolynomial.homogeneousSubmodule (Fin N) k) MvPolynomial.gradedAlgebra) ≤ q) :
    ∃ U : Subalgebra k (MvPolynomial (Fin N) k),
      (∀ f : MvPolynomial (Fin N) k, f ∈ U ↔
        ∀ d : ℕ, algebraMap (MvPolynomial (Fin N) k) (Localization.AtPrime q)
          (MvPolynomial.homogeneousComponent d f) ∈
            q.map (algebraMap (MvPolynomial (Fin N) k) (Localization.AtPrime q)) ^ d) ∧
      (@Submodule.IsHomogeneous _ _ _ _ _ _ _ U.toSubmodule
        (MvPolynomial.homogeneousSubmodule (Fin N) k) _ _ _ MvPolynomial.decomposition) ∧
      U = Algebra.adjoin k {t : MvPolynomial (Fin N) k |
        t ∈ U ∧ (∃ d : ℕ, 0 < d ∧ t.IsHomogeneous d) ∧
          MvPolynomial.aeval
              (fun i : Fin N ↦
                (MvPolynomial.X (Sum.inl i) : MvPolynomial (Fin N ⊕ Fin N) k) +
                  MvPolynomial.X (Sum.inr i)) t =
            MvPolynomial.rename (Sum.inl : Fin N → Fin N ⊕ Fin N) t +
              MvPolynomial.rename (Sum.inr : Fin N → Fin N ⊕ Fin N) t} := by
  sorry

end Hironaka
